ARCHITECTURE - Theoretical


1. High-Level Architecture (Logical)

Think of Phase 2 as four layers:

1. Source Systems
SAP (orders, confirmations, material movements, BOM, costing)
PDS (packaging attributes, dimensions, weights)
QA / LIMS (SG, viscosity, QC attributes)
TML / OT / MES (cycle times, micro-stops, downtimes, speed)
Pack Assist (change parts, line–format compatibility, NPL setup)
Existing GS outputs (Phase 1 benchmarks)

2. Data & Integration Layer
EDL / ADLS Gen2 landing zone
Ingestion pipelines (batch, possibly some near real-time for TML)
Schema validation & standardization

3. Processing & Analytics Layer (Databricks)

• Medallion architecture: Bronze (raw) → Silver (cleaned) → Gold (business model)
• Feature engineering for:
• Overfill optimization
• NPL prediction
• Sequencing optimization
• ML / OR models:
• Overfill risk model
• NPL performance model
• Sequencing optimization engine

4. Consumption / Experience Layer
• Power BI / Pulse dashboards (Overfill, NPL, Sequencing, GS)
• APIs / views to PackAssist & Pulse UI
• ELLA / semantic layer for natural-language queries (optional but in your doc set)


2. Source → Staging Design (Granular)
2.1 SAP – Core Tables (Production & Variance)

Main objects:
• AFKO – Order header (production orders)
• AFVC – Operations (routing steps inside the order)
• AFRU – Confirmations (actuals: qty, time)
• MSEG / MKPF – Material movements (actual consumption, scrap)
• RESB – Reservations (planned component usage)
• STPO / STKO – BOM header and items
• CRHD – Work centers (lines, machines)
• MBEW / KEKO – Costing (standard cost, variances)

Staging grain (Silver layer):
• stg_production_order (from AFKO)
• Key: AUFNR (Order), WERKS (Plant)
• stg_production_operation (from AFVC)
• Key: AUFNR, APLZL (internal operation counter)
• stg_production_confirmation (from AFRU)
• Key: AUFNR, RUECK (confirmation), RMZHL (counter)
• stg_material_movement (from MSEG + MKPF)
• Key: MBLNR, ZEILE (doc+item)
• stg_reservation (from RESB)
• stg_bom_item (from STPO)
• stg_workcenter (from CRHD)
• stg_costing (from MBEW/KEKO)

Everything at this layer is 1:1 with SAP tables, plus basic harmonization (data types, date formats).


2.2 PDS – Packaging & Format Data

Concept:
• Gives you package geometry and component weights, key for NPL + Overfill + Sequencing.

Staging:
• stg_pds_package
• PACKAGE_ID
• MATNR (SKU material)
• HEIGHT, WIDTH, DEPTH, DIAMETER, VOLUME
• MATERIAL_TYPE (plastic, glass, tube, jar)
• EMPTY_WEIGHT, LABEL_WEIGHT, CLOSURE_WEIGHT
• TOLERANCE_CLASS (if defined)


2.3 QA / LIMS – SG & Viscosity

Staging tables:
• stg_lims_sg
• MATERIAL_ID, BATCH_ID, SG_VALUE, MEASURE_DATE, TEMP
• stg_lims_rheology
• MATERIAL_ID, BATCH_ID, VISCOSITY_CLASS, VISCOSITY_VALUE

These feed SG-adjusted overfill and segmentation of products into viscosity classes (impacts line behavior).


2.4 TML / OT – Time Loss & Speed

Staging tables:
• stg_tml_cycle_time
• PLANT, LINE_ID, SKU_ID, DATE, SHIFT, AVG_CYCLETIME
• stg_tml_events
• PLANT, LINE_ID, EVENT_TYPE (downtime, microstop), START_TS, END_TS

These feed:
• Line speed features
• Downtime patterns
• Sequencing constraints (downtime windows)


2.5 PackAssist – Change Parts & Line Compatibility

Staging tables:
• stg_packassist_change_parts
• LINE_ID, FORMAT_ID, CHANGE_PART_SET, SETUP_TIME_STD
• stg_packassist_compatibility
• LINE_ID, SKU_ID, IS_COMPATIBLE, NOTES
• stg_packassist_npl_setup
• NPL_ID, REQUIRED_PARTS, PREFERRED_LINES

These are critical for NPL feasible line selection and sequencing changeover matrix.

3. Canonical Data Model (Gold Layer)

Now the part vendors must really understand: your canonical model.

3.1 Dimensions

DimPlant
• PlantKey (surrogate)
• PlantCode (WERKS)
• PlantName
• Region, Country

DimLine
• LineKey
• LineCode (from CRHD / TML)
• PlantKey
• LineType (Fill & Assembly, Packaging)
• CapacityClass (High-speed, Medium, etc.)

DimProductSKU
• ProductKey
• MaterialID (MATNR)
• Brand
• Category
• Subcategory
• NPLFlag
• Link to GS anchor (from Phase 1)

DimPackage
• PackageKey
• MaterialID (container material)
• FormatType (bottle, jar, tube, pump)
• Volume_Nominal
• EmptyWeight
• TotalPackageWeight
• ToleranceClass

DimBatch
• BatchKey
• MaterialID
• BatchID
• ManufactureDate

DimTime
• DateKey (YYYYMMDD)
• Date, Month, Year, Week, Quarter
• Shift-level: ShiftCode, ShiftStart, ShiftEnd (or separate DimShift)

DimOperator (optional, where data exists)
• OperatorKey
• OperatorID
• SkillLevel, Role

DimNPL
• NPLKey
• NPLCode
• ProjectName
• TargetLaunchDate
• TargetLine


3.2 Fact Tables

3.2.1 FactProductionExecution

Grain:
One row per Order × Operation × Date × Line × SKU (optionally batch).

Keys:
• PlantKey
• LineKey
• ProductKey
• BatchKey (if available)
• TimeKey
• OrderID (AUFNR)
• OperationID (APLZL or work center routing step)

Measures:
• PlannedQty
• ConfirmedQty
• ScrapQty
• ProducedQty
• ConfirmedMachineTime
• ConfirmedLaborTime
• OEE (calculated)
• Speed_Actual
• Speed_GS_Target (joined from GS table)

This is the core fact your GS + NPL + Sequencing will rely on.


3.2.2 FactMaterialConsumption

Grain:
One row per Order × Component Material × Batch (derived from MSEG + RESB + STPO).

Keys:
• PlantKey
• OrderID
• ProductKey (header)
• ComponentMaterialKey
• BatchKey
• TimeKey

Measures:
• PlannedQty_Component
• ActualQty_Component
• VarianceQty
• VarianceCost (ActualQty – PlannedQty) × StandardCost

This is the source for overfill & scrap variance.


3.2.3 FactOverfill (Derived Mart)

This is not a raw table from SAP; it’s a Phase 2 computed mart.

Grain (recommended):
One row per SKU × Line × Shift × Day or even per SKU × Line × Batch where data supports.

Keys:
• PlantKey
• LineKey
• ProductKey
• PackageKey
• BatchKey (optional)
• TimeKey

Measures (VERY IMPORTANT):
• NominalVolume
• RegulatoryMinFill
• TargetFillWeight = NominalVolume × SG (from LIMS)
• AverageActualFillWeight (from material consumption / in-line measurements if available)
• OverfillWeight = AverageActualFillWeight – TargetFillWeight
• OverfillPercent = OverfillWeight / TargetFillWeight
• OverfillCost = OverfillWeight × MaterialUnitCost
• OverfillStdDev (σ)
• Cp, CpK per SKU/line


Stat fields:
• UpperControlLimit, LowerControlLimit
• OverfillFlag (above UCL yes/no)

This is what powers:
• Overfill dashboard
• Predictive features
• Overfill ranking & watchlists


3.2.4 FactScrap
Grain:
One row per Order × SKU × ScrapReason × Day.

Keys:
• PlantKey
• LineKey
• ProductKey
• TimeKey
• ScrapReasonKey (from SAP QA or custom codes)

Measures:
• ScrapQty
• ScrapCost (ScrapQty × StandardCost)
• ScrapRate (ScrapQty / ProducedQty)

Useful for:
• NPL risk features
• Overfill vs scrap interplay
• Sequencing risk profile


3.2.5 FactChangeoverMatrix

Grain:
One row per Line × FromSKU × ToSKU (optionally plant-level).

Keys:
• PlantKey
• LineKey
• FromProductKey
• ToProductKey

Measures:
• AvgChangeoverTime
• StdDevChangeoverTime
• ChangeoverType (format change, minor change, full wash)
• CleaningRequiredFlag

Source: derived from historical order sequences + downtime/TML events + PackAssist.

This is the backbone of sequencing optimization.


3.2.6 Fact NPLPrediction

Grain:
One row per NPL × Line × Scenario.

Keys:
• NPLKey
• LineKey
• ScenarioID

Measures:
• PredictedOEE
• PredictedSpeed
• PredictedScrapRate
• PredictedOverfillRisk
• LaunchReadinessScore
• RiskCategory (Low/Medium/High)

This fact is fed by the NPL ML model and displayed in NPL dashboards and PackAssist.

3.2.7 FactSequenceScenario

Grain:
One row per Line × Shift × Scenario × SequencePosition.

Keys:
• PlantKey
• LineKey
• TimeKey (date)
• ShiftKey
• ScenarioID
• SequencePosition (1, 2, 3…)

Attributes:
• ProductKey (SKU at that position)
• OrderID or AggregatedOrderGroupID

Measures:
• ExpectedThroughput
• ExpectedChangeoverTimeFromPrevious
• CumulativeChangeoverTime
• ExpectedOEE
• ScenarioScore (global objective function)

This underpins the Gantt UI and scenario comparison in sequencing.


4. Feature Tables for ML / Optimization

To talk to data science / vendor ML teams, give them this mental model:

4.1 Overfill Feature Table: feat_overfill

Grain:
SKU × Line × Batch or SKU × Line × Shift × Day.

Columns (examples):
• Keys: PlantKey, LineKey, ProductKey, BatchKey, TimeKey
• Features:
• SG_Value
• ViscosityClass
• Speed_Planned, Speed_Actual
• OEE
• ScrapRate
• AvgFillWeight
• ContainerVolume
• EmptyContainerWeight
• OperatorID (if available)
• AmbientTemp (if available from OT)
• Label:
• OverfillFlag (1 if above tolerance band)
• Or continuous OverfillPercent


4.2 NPL Feature Table: feat_npl

Grain:
Historical SKUs used to train the model.

Columns:
• Packaging features (dimensions, material, format)
• GS performance features (GS_OEE, GS_Speed, GS_Scrap)
• Complexity indicators (No. of components, label complexity, decoration)
• Changeover complexity (avg changeover time when switching to/from this SKU)
• Outcome labels:
• Actual OEE
• ActualScrapRate
• StabilizationTimeAfterLaunch

For each new NPL, the same feature set is derived using planned packaging data, and the model predicts performance.


4.3 Sequencing Feature Table: feat_sequence

Grain:
Pairs of SKU sequences and historical performance.

Columns:
• LineKey
• FromProductKey, ToProductKey
• ChangeoverTime
• ChangeoverType
• CO_Cost
• ThroughputAfterChangeover
• DowntimeFlag (if cleaning required)
• GS_Delta (distance from GS after changeover)

This set feeds the OR engine and can also be used to learn good/bad changeover patterns.

